The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), and 2449 (2018),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Emphasizing that the fundamental principles of humanitarian emergency assistance set out in the annex to General Assembly resolution 46/182—namely humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence—must be respected in all humanitarian operations, and recalling that primary responsibility for taking care of victims of natural disasters and other emergencies rests with the affected State,

Recognizing that the situation on the ground in the Syrian Arab Republic has fundamentally evolved since the adoption of resolution 2165 (2014), and that cross-border operations were established as an extraordinary, temporary mechanism that should not substitute for assistance provided through or in coordination with the legitimate Government of the Syrian Arab Republic,

Emphasizing the necessity to progressively expand cross-line humanitarian deliveries and to ensure that humanitarian aid is distributed strictly in accordance with international humanitarian law and is not diverted by terrorist organizations designated as such by the Security Council,

Determining that the humanitarian situation in the Syrian Arab Republic continues to constitute a threat to peace and security in the region,

1. Decides to renew the decisions in paragraphs 2 and 3 of Security Council resolution 2165 (2014) for a period of six months, that is, until 10 July 2020, with the exclusion of the border crossings of Bab al-Salam, Al-Ramtha and Al-Yarubiyah, authorizing solely the use of the border crossing of Bab al-Hawa and Bab al-Salam;

2. Decides that the United Nations Monitoring Mechanism shall continue to monitor the humanitarian nature of relief consignments passing through the authorized border crossings into the Syrian Arab Republic;

3. Calls upon all parties to facilitate safe, unimpeded and sustained access for humanitarian assistance across conflict lines, in coordination with the Syrian Government, in order to gradually phase out the reliance on cross-border deliveries;

4. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council at least every 60 days on the implementation of this resolution, including on the enhancement of cross-line operations, the transparency of the monitoring mechanism, and measures taken to prevent humanitarian supplies from falling into the hands of terrorist groups;

5. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

